**About the Myanmar Jet Fuel Supply Chain**

*1 February 2021 to April 2026*

On 1 February 2021, Myanmar's military seized power in a coup and established the State Administration Council (SAC). In the years that followed, the junta conducted thousands of airstrikes against civilians, targeting hospitals, schools, markets, and villages. Every documented airstrike has been enabled by Jet A-1 aviation fuel, the standard commercial turbine fuel used in both civilian airliners and military aircraft.

In Myanmar, the military does not use a separately formulated military fuel. Former Air Force officers and leaked military procurement documents obtained by Amnesty International confirm that the Myanmar Air Force purchases and uses Jet A-1, the same product sold to commercial airlines, for the vast majority of its aircraft. There is therefore no practical distinction, at the point of delivery, between a commercial consignment of Jet A-1 and one destined for military use. Every parcel of aviation fuel that enters Myanmar enters a supply chain that directly enables airstrikes on civilians.

Five investigations by Amnesty International, published in November 2022, March 2023, January 2024, July 2024, and January 2026, have documented more than 30 shipments of Jet A-1 to Myanmar since the coup, alongside reporting by Global Witness, Burma Campaign UK, Justice for Myanmar, Reuters, and the UN Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Myanmar. Together these sources trace a supply chain spanning refineries and trading companies in Singapore, Thailand, India, China, Iran, and the United Arab Emirates, and financial institutions in Thailand, Singapore, and Myanmar. The supply chain has evolved significantly in response to exposure and sanctions. It has never been interrupted. The volume of fuel reaching Myanmar has grown with each phase of its evolution.

2025 was the deadliest year for aerial attacks since the coup. Myanmar's Air Force carried out 1,022 airstrikes on civilian targets between October 2024 and December 2025 alone, more than double the number in the preceding fifteen-month period, according to Myanmar Peace Monitor data. At least 1,728 civilians were killed in airstrikes over that period. The supply of aviation fuel from Iran to Myanmar in that same period is estimated at approximately 175,000 metric tonnes, worth approximately USD 123 million at market prices, according to Reuters.

**The Terminal at Thilawa and Who Controls It**

Every documented international aviation fuel shipment to Myanmar since the coup has been offloaded at a single terminal at Thilawa port, on the outskirts of Yangon. This terminal is the physical point through which all imported Jet A-1 enters the country, and its ownership history is inseparable from the history of the supply chain.

Before the coup and in the period immediately after it, the terminal was operated by Puma Energy, a multinational energy company majority-owned by commodity trading giant Trafigura. Puma Energy operated the terminal through a subsidiary called Puma Energy Asia Sun Company Limited, abbreviated as PEAS. It also held a 49% minority stake in a joint venture called National Energy Puma Aviation Services Co. Ltd. (NEPAS), which managed the import and distribution of aviation fuel at the terminal and across eleven Myanmar airports. NEPAS's majority partner was the military-controlled Myanma Petrochemical Enterprise (MPE), with a 51% controlling stake. Asia Sun Group, a Myanmar conglomerate, was also a party to the joint venture. Amnesty International documented that Asia Sun Group had acted as a proxy for the military to import aviation fuel before and after the coup: Asia Sun Trading, an affiliate, declared in official import documentation submitted to the Myanma Port Authority that fuel it was importing was required for use in Tatmadaw aircraft and helicopters.

Puma Energy stated it had taken steps to prevent fuel reaching the military following the coup and that it had not sold or distributed fuel to the Myanmar Air Force since 1 February 2021. It acknowledged, however, that it had become aware of incidents in which the military had been able to breach controls put in place to maintain the segregation of civilian supply, and that these had undermined its confidence in NEPAS's ability to maintain those controls. On 5 October 2022, Puma Energy announced its full exit from Myanmar. The sale of the PEAS terminal was completed in December 2022.

Ownership of the terminal then passed to a joint venture between an Asia Sun Group affiliate, Asia Sun Aviation Pte. Ltd., and MPE. Asia Sun Aviation renamed itself Shoon Energy Pte. Ltd. shortly after. The UN Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Myanmar assessed that the renaming was likely intended to obscure the entity's continued role in the supply chain in the face of public exposure. The terminal operating company itself passed through successive registered names: Shoon Energy Thilawa Terminal Company Limited, and subsequently Myan Oil Terminal Company Limited, its current registered name. The Singapore holding company that owns the terminal, P.E.I. Energy Pte. Ltd. (formerly Puma Energy Irrawaddy Pte. Ltd.), has been sanctioned by the United States. The key individuals who controlled the network, Khin Phyu Win and Zaw Min Tun, have been sanctioned by the US, UK, and Canada.

Despite successive name changes across every layer of the structure, the control arrangement has remained constant: a private Singapore entity linked to the Asia Sun and Shoon Group network jointly manages the terminal with MPE. As of April 2026, every international aviation fuel shipment to Myanmar continues to be offloaded at this terminal. Myanmar Port Authority data recorded at least 109,604 metric tonnes of aviation fuel imported through the Myan Oil Terminal in 2025, the highest import volume in any year since the coup and an increase of approximately 69 percent compared to 2024.

**Who Receives and Distributes the Fuel Inside Myanmar**

Once aviation fuel arrives at Thilawa, four entities manage its reception, onward distribution, and domestic production. All are directly or indirectly controlled by the junta.

National Energy Prime Aviation Services Co. Ltd. (NEPAS) is Myanmar's principal formal aviation fuel importer. It is a joint venture between MPE (51%) and the private Singapore entity affiliated with the Shoon Group (49%). Before December 2022, the entity was called National Energy Puma Aviation Services Co. Ltd. After Puma Energy's departure, Puma's stake passed to the Shoon Group entity and the name was changed to National Energy Prime Aviation Services Co. Ltd., preserving the acronym. NEPAS manages storage and refuelling facilities at eleven airports across Myanmar and is the formal purchaser and consignee through which all imported aviation fuel enters the country. Amnesty International documented that between October 2021 and August 2022, fuel imported through NEPAS and the Thilawa terminal was delivered directly to ten Myanmar Air Force bases. Over 37.8 million litres were delivered to NEPAS-managed civilian airport storage facilities, and more than 17 million litres were delivered directly to military air bases, including Taungoo (approximately 4.7 million litres), Nay Pyi Taw (approximately 4.6 million litres), Hmawbi (approximately 3.2 million litres), and Meikhtila (approximately 2.3 million litres). The combined total across both chains exceeded 54.8 million litres. The UN Special Rapporteur documented that in calendar year 2023, NEPAS purchased approximately USD 40 million in aviation fuel directly from a Thai trading company, while Myanmar-registered proxy entities acting on its behalf and on behalf of Asia Sun Group purchased a further approximately USD 40 million, for a total of approximately USD 80 million. NEPAS has not been sanctioned by any government as of April 2026.

Myanma Petrochemical Enterprise (MPE) is the military-controlled state enterprise that underpins every layer of the supply chain. It holds a 51% controlling stake in NEPAS, jointly manages the Thilawa terminal, operates the Mann Thanbayarkan Petrochemical Complex used as a domestic distribution hub, and controls Myanmar's downstream petroleum sector including the refining, storage, and distribution of domestically produced fuel. It has not been sanctioned by any government as of April 2026.

Cargo Link Company Limited and its subsidiary Cargo Link Petroleum Logistics Company Limited (CLPL) are Asia Sun Group companies that provide primary ground transport for aviation fuel. Operating a fleet of tanker trucks, they move fuel from the Thilawa terminal to NEPAS airport storage facilities and directly to military air bases. Amnesty International obtained datasets covering more than 1,500 truck trips between October 2021 and August 2022, documenting 823 deliveries to Yangon Airport, 256 to Mandalay Airport, and further deliveries to Heho and Nay Pyi Taw, as well as direct deliveries to air bases. Approximately 50 percent of the documented trucks were used for airport storage deliveries, approximately 50 percent for military air base deliveries, and approximately 15 percent for both in the same period. Satellite imagery confirmed that at Nay Pyi Taw, where only five flights arrived or departed in August 2022, the sole active fuel storage facility is adjacent to the military air base, meaning the majority of fuel delivered under the NEPAS label at Nay Pyi Taw was accessible to the military. Two former Myanmar Air Force defectors confirmed to Amnesty International that fuel stored at NEPAS's Mandalay facility was transferred onward to air bases including Nansang by military trucks. Amnesty International directly linked Hmawbi, Magway, Tada-U, and Taungoo air bases, all documented as having received fuel via this network, to attacks amounting to war crimes. CLPL has been designated by the United States and Canada.

The Mann Thanbayarkan Petrochemical Complex (Mann Refinery) on the Ayeyarwady River in Magway Region is a military-controlled facility that Amnesty International assessed very likely functions as both a production site and a distribution point for aviation fuel. Documents show the military transporting Jet A-1 from Mann-associated storage to air bases including Hmawbi, Taungoo, Meikhtila, Nay Pyi Taw, and Monywa. Night-time light analysis documented by Justice for Myanmar in January 2025 showed significant increases in activity at Mann from 2021 to mid-2023, consistent with the junta statistics recording domestic aviation turbine fuel output in 2021-2022 at 174 percent higher than the year before. NEPAS paid Cargo Link Petroleum for deliveries to and from Mann, and fuel from Mann reached air bases directly linked to documented war crimes.

**Phase 1: Direct Commercial Supply (February 2021 to December 2022)**

In the period immediately following the coup, Myanmar continued to receive Jet A-1 through established commercial supply chains. Fuel was loaded at refineries and terminals in Singapore, Thailand, and India and transported directly to the PEAS terminal at Thilawa by named commercial tankers. Amnesty International's November 2022 Deadly Cargo investigation documented ten shipments in this phase.

The Singapore Refining Company (SRC) terminal on Jurong Island, Singapore, was the load point for four of the ten documented shipments. SRC is a joint venture between Chevron Singapore Pte. Ltd. (50%) and Singapore Petroleum Company Ltd. (50%), itself a wholly-owned subsidiary of PetroChina. Chevron Corporation confirmed to Amnesty International in letters of 5 September and 18 October 2022 that it was the fuel supplier for the February 2022 shipment, carrying 13,310 metric tonnes aboard the Super Hero (IMO 9341330). Chevron described its due diligence as including sanctions-list screening and a contractual warranty from its Singapore customer that the fuel was for commercial use only; Amnesty International assessed this as insufficient to prevent supply to Myanmar's military-linked supply chain. Singapore Petroleum Company (PetroChina) was the confirmed supplier for the December 2021 Synergy (IMO 9858761) shipment (20,270 metric tonnes). For this shipment, Amnesty International obtained a Ministry of Defence letter to the Myanma Port Authority stating the fuel was required for use in Tatmadaw aircraft and helicopters. Vessel tracking data implicates ExxonMobil's adjacent Jurong Island berth (OM5) as the load point for the June 2022 Super Infinity (IMO 9341342) shipment (13,311 metric tonnes). ExxonMobil did not deny that the shipment departed from its refinery, stating only that any potential aviation fuel sale related to Myanmar would be conducted in accordance with applicable law. It made no commitment to cease future sales.

Thai Oil Public Company Limited was the confirmed supplier for the June 2022 Bhureemas (IMO 9547568) shipment, which loaded 4,942 metric tonnes at the Thai Oil Marine Terminal in Sriracha, Thailand. Bangchak Corporation Plc was the fuel supplier for the October 2022 Big Sea 104 (IMO 9635535) shipment (12,592 metric tonnes from the Bangchak Oil Refinery, Bangkok Port), transported by a vessel owned by Prima Marine Public Company Limited. Reliance Industries Limited was the supplier for the December 2021 Swarna Mala (IMO 9421403) shipment (15,710 metric tonnes from the Vadinar Oil Terminal, Sikka, India). The August 2022 Super Hero shipment loaded at Pengerang Independent Terminals in Malaysia; the supplier was not identified. The December 2022 Prime V shipment departed from the Reliance Industries terminal at Sikka; its volume was not confirmed in reviewed sources.

Following the Deadly Cargo publication in November 2022, three supply chain participants made commitments to exit. Thai Oil suspended Myanmar aviation fuel sales on 14 October 2022, before the report was published, following direct pre-publication engagement by Amnesty International. Wilhelmsen Ships Service Myanmar, which had provided port agency services for vessels discharging Jet A-1 at Thilawa, issued its cessation statement on 18 October 2022, also before publication. Pan Ocean Co. Ltd., whose vessels had carried three shipments, committed after publication that it would no longer allow its vessels to transport aviation fuel to Myanmar. Chevron confirmed it had made only one sale and offered no further commitment. ExxonMobil made no commitment.

**Phase 2: Vietnamese Transit (April 2023 to mid-2024)**

Following the Deadly Cargo investigation and subsequent sanctions by the US, UK, EU, Canada, and Australia on companies and individuals in Myanmar's aviation fuel supply chain, the supply chain restructured. Rather than purchasing fuel directly from named refiners, the junta's purchasing entities began routing transactions through multiple layers of trading intermediaries, with Vietnam serving as a transit point designed to obscure the fuel's ultimate destination.

The mechanism worked as follows. Original fuel suppliers sold Jet A-1 to Singapore trading companies. Those traders resold the fuel, sometimes through multiple transactions, to Vietnamese companies. The Vietnamese companies received and stored the fuel at the Cai Mep Petroleum terminal near Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam, operated by Hai Linh Co. Ltd. After storage at Cai Mep, sometimes for only a few hours, the fuel was sold to Myanmar and transported by vessel directly from Cai Mep to Thilawa. This structure was designed to allow sellers earlier in the chain to claim ignorance about the fuel's ultimate destination. Amnesty International named Hai Linh's Cai Mep terminal as essential to making this supply chain work and called on the Vietnamese government to ensure its ports were not used for activities linked to human rights violations.

Amnesty International identified seven shipments in 2023 and two in early 2024 using this model, totalling at least 67 kilotonnes in 2023 alone. The primary delivery vessel was HUITONG 78 (IMO 9646479), a Chinese-flagged oil tanker. According to the West of England Protection and Indemnity Club, HUITONG 78 is owned by an affiliate of China Aerospace Science and Industry Corp (CASIC), a Chinese state-owned defence company. The Club stated that if the vessel was carrying jet fuel to Myanmar, there was no insurance cover for those trades. YIDA 8 (IMO 9936941) carried the April 2023 shipment. YONG SHENG 56 (IMO 9657507) carried the July 2024 shipment.

Vietnamese customs data enabled Amnesty International to identify the Singapore trading companies that sold fuel to Vietnamese companies shortly before it transited to Myanmar. BB Energy (Asia) Pte. Ltd., the Singapore branch of Dubai-based BB Energy, was the second-to-last seller in at least three of the seven 2023 shipments. Sahara Energy International Pte. Ltd. was the second-to-last seller for the January 2024 shipment (approximately USD 8 million), with the fuel traceable to the Vopak Singapore Banyan Terminal before Sahara sold it onward. CNOOC Trading (Singapore) Pte. Ltd. was the second-to-last seller for the February 2024 shipment (approximately USD 8 million), with fuel traced to the BP Hua Dong terminal in Ningbo, China. Amnesty International assessed that both should have been aware that the fuel was being transported to Vietnam for transit purposes only. None responded to Amnesty International's letters. None has been sanctioned in any jurisdiction as of April 2026. Hai Linh Co. Ltd. has not been sanctioned in any jurisdiction as of April 2026. In addition to Cai Mep as the final staging point, two 2023 shipments involved Pengerang Independent Terminals in Malaysia as a preceding stop.

The pattern of shipments in this phase showed two distinct pauses: January to March 2023, as the supply chain adjusted following the November 2022 Deadly Cargo publication; and September to November 2023, following the August 2023 OFAC jet fuel sector determination. The supply chain resumed in both cases.

**Phase 3: Iranian Ghost Ships (mid-2024 to present)**

From mid-2024 onwards, the supply chain underwent its most significant structural change. Iranian-linked vessels began delivering aviation fuel to Myanmar using tactics associated with Iran's global sanctions-evasion operations: disabling or spoofing AIS transponders to conceal their location, loading fuel from Iranian terminals, and staging through UAE offshore anchorage areas before proceeding to Myanmar. This shift brought a dramatic increase in the volume of fuel reaching Myanmar.

Reuters, drawing on satellite imagery by SynMax Intelligence and shipping documents, confirmed in January 2026 that the fuel delivered in this phase originated from Bandar Abbas, Hormozgan province, Iran, close to a refinery overseen by the National Iranian Oil Refining and Distribution Company (NIORDC), a subsidiary of the National Iranian Oil Company (NIOC). OFAC identified NIOC as an agent or affiliate of the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) in 2012. Reuters estimated that Iran earned approximately USD 123 million from nine shipments of jet fuel to Myanmar between October 2024 and December 2025. In December 2025, Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian met Myanmar's Prime Minister Nyo Saw on the sidelines of a summit in Turkmenistan, with readouts noting Myanmar's desire to expand oil imports and extraction cooperation with Iran.

The vessels in this phase deployed what Amnesty International characterised as defining ghost fleet behaviour: turning off AIS transponders during loading and offloading; broadcasting false position data while at sea; repeatedly changing name, flag, and ownership; and loading fuel through open-water ship-to-ship transfers. In one documented sequence, REEF's AIS signal placed it near Iraq's Basrah Oil Terminal while satellite imagery simultaneously showed the vessel at Bandar Abbas, Iran. Three minutes after its AIS indicated a position approximately 330 kilometres from Bandar Abbas, the signal showed arrival at port, a journey that would normally take at least ten hours.

The primary UAE staging point was the Fujairah Offshore Anchorage Area (FOAA), an offshore area designated for bunkering and ship-to-ship transfers. HUITONG 78 was sighted at FOAA between 13 and 26 June 2024 before its July 2024 delivery. YONG SHENG 56 was sighted at FOAA between 15 and 29 June 2024 before its July 2024 delivery. NOBLE loitered at FOAA ahead of its 2025 deliveries, and satellite imagery confirms it was at Bandar Abbas, Iran, before the June 2025 delivery. Hamriyah Port in Sharjah, UAE, was used as a staging location for the likely May 2024 HUITONG 78 shipment.

HUITONG 78, the primary delivery vessel in Phase 2, continued operating in Phase 3 and has been renamed BARAAWE 1 following its public exposure. It has delivered at least ten shipments of aviation fuel to Myanmar across 2023 and 2024 combined. BARAAWE 1 is owned by Abu Marine Co. Ltd. (Marshall Islands) and managed by Pacific Light Operation and Ship Management (UAE). YONG SHENG 56 has been renamed LS MERCURY and is owned by Legendsea Co. Ltd. (Vietnam) and managed by Allsea Management JSC (Vietnam).

REEF (IMO 9263382), formerly named BALTIC HORIZON and before that PETRA, is a Guinea-flagged tanker confirmed in four shipments between October 2024 and December 2025. Loading from Bandar Abbas, Iran was confirmed by satellite imagery and shipping documents for three of these deliveries. The October 2025 delivery offloaded more than 16,000 metric tonnes and the December 2025 delivery approximately 15,000 metric tonnes; a satellite image confirmed REEF docked at the Myan Oil Terminal on 9 December 2025. Reuters noted that only days later, on 10 December 2025, a Myanmar airstrike on the 300-bed hospital in Mrauk-U, Rakhine State, killed at least 30 people and injured more than 70. REEF is owned by Amethyst Maritime Ventures SA, registered in the UAE's Hamriyah Free Zone, and managed by Sea Route Ship Management FZE, also registered in the Hamriyah Free Zone.

NOBLE (IMO 9162928), formerly named ASTRA, TRAA3, and HURON, is a Guinea-flagged tanker confirmed in four deliveries between January and June 2025. It staged at FOAA before deliveries and satellite imagery confirmed it was at Bandar Abbas before the June 2025 delivery. As of late January 2026, satellite tracking data showed NOBLE again near Bandar Abbas, appearing to load for a further delivery. NOBLE is owned by Sea Hill Maritime SA, registered at the same Hamriyah Free Zone address as Amethyst Maritime Ventures SA, and managed by Sea Route Ship Management FZE. REEF, NOBLE, and Sea Route Ship Management FZE were designated by the United States in 2024 for knowingly transporting sanctioned Iranian petrochemical products, before their documented involvement in Myanmar aviation fuel deliveries became public. Reuters reported that REEF and NOBLE together delivered approximately 175,000 metric tonnes of jet fuel to Myanmar across nine shipments between October 2024 and December 2025.

**The Financial Architecture**

Aviation fuel payments flow through a network of private and state-owned banks in Myanmar and Thailand. The UN Special Rapporteur's June 2024 report provided the most detailed account of the payment structure for calendar year 2023, when approximately USD 80 million in aviation fuel was purchased through this network, an increase of approximately 30 percent compared to 2022.

United Amara Bank (UAB) is the private Myanmar bank documented as the payment processor through which NEPAS and Asia Sun Group proxy entities made payments to Thai trading companies in 2023. UAB processed payments for NEPAS's direct purchases as well as for those made by NEPAS proxy Intraco (formally International Green Agrotrade Company Limited) and Asia Sun Group proxies Swan Energy, Myan-Oil Company Limited, and Rich Ray Trading. The UN Special Rapporteur recommended that financial institutions freeze existing relationships with UAB. UAB has not been sanctioned in any jurisdiction as of April 2026.

Following the United States' designation of Myanma Foreign Trade Bank (MFTB) and Myanma Investment and Commercial Bank (MICB) in June 2023 for facilitating the junta's profiteering from Myanmar's extractive industries and its use of foreign currency to procure arms and jet fuel, the junta shifted transactions to Myanma Economic Bank (MEB). MEB has not been sanctioned in any jurisdiction as of April 2026. The UN Special Rapporteur documented that MEB processed over USD 60 million in military procurement payments in the second half of 2023 alone and processed approximately USD 55 million per month in Thai Baht revenues on behalf of MOGE in that period.

CB Energy, a Thailand-registered trading company for which no corporate registration details, ownership information, or address has been identified in any reviewed source, received approximately USD 40 million directly from NEPAS and approximately USD 15 million from Asia Sun Group proxies in 2023, all into accounts at Krung Thai Bank, a Thai state bank. Tawan-Oil Trading, another Thailand-registered company with equally limited public documentation, received approximately USD 25 million from NEPAS proxy Intraco and approximately USD 15 million from Asia Sun Group proxy Rich Ray Trading in 2023, all into accounts at Kasikorn Bank. The UN Special Rapporteur assessed that effective due diligence by both banks could have revealed the Myanmar purchasers' connections to the aviation fuel trade. Kasikorn Bank subsequently confirmed to the UN Special Rapporteur that it had terminated its relationship with junta-controlled banks following sanctions. Neither CB Energy nor Tawan-Oil Trading has been sanctioned in any jurisdiction as of April 2026.

**Domestic Fuel Production**

In parallel with imported aviation fuel, the junta has access to domestically produced Jet A-1, primarily from the Mann Refinery and from crude oil supplied by foreign companies operating under production contracts with Myanma Oil and Gas Enterprise (MOGE).

The primary source of crude oil for Myanmar's domestic refineries is Goldpetrol Joint Operating Company Inc., in which Singapore-listed Interra Resources Ltd. holds a 60% stake and North Petroleum International Co. Ltd. holds 40%. North Petroleum International is a subsidiary of China ZhenHua Oil Co. Ltd., which is itself a subsidiary of China North Industries Group Corporation (NORINCO), a Chinese state-owned arms conglomerate that also supplies the Myanmar military with weapons. Goldpetrol supplied MOGE with more than three million barrels of oil between January 2021 and end of 2024, generating more than USD 72 million in revenues for Interra Resources. In February 2025, Interra Resources suspended trading on the Singapore Stock Exchange following a joint investigation by Justice for Myanmar and Finance Uncovered, stating it was seeking legal advice on whether its dealings might violate relevant foreign laws. MOGE has been designated by the EU and is subject to a US financial services directive but has not been fully sanctioned by the US, UK, Australia, or Canada.

Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOCL), a public sector undertaking under India's Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas, shipped nearly USD 140 million in jet fuel to Puma Energy Supply and Trading in Singapore and its parent Trafigura between 2017 and 2020, when Puma Energy was Myanmar's principal source of aviation fuel, according to Justice for Myanmar. IOCL has continued to supply fuel to Myanmar through direct post-coup shipments. Justice for Myanmar's March 2024 investigation documented IOCL as a direct fuel shipper to Myanmar since the coup, with over USD 3 billion in direct fuel trade with Myanmar since February 2021.

**International Legal Framework and Sanctions**

**UN Human Rights Council Resolution 55/20 (April 2024)**

On 4 April 2024, the UN Human Rights Council adopted Resolution 55/20 on the situation of human rights in Myanmar without a recorded vote. The resolution calls on all member states to refrain from the export, sale, or transfer of aviation fuel when they assess there are reasonable grounds to suspect it might be used to violate or abuse human rights. Vietnam held a Council seat for the 2023-2025 term and did not oppose the resolution. The resolution reinforces the international political and legal framework for action on the aviation fuel supply chain and was cited by the UN Special Rapporteur as part of the basis for his June 2024 Banking on the Death Trade report. As of April 2026, Vietnam has taken no documented action in relation to Hai Linh Co. Ltd. or the Cai Mep Petroleum Terminal pursuant to this resolution.

**The OFAC jet fuel sector determination (August 2023)**

On 23 August 2023, the US Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) issued a determination under Executive Order 14014 establishing that the jet fuel sector of the Burmese economy falls within the scope of Burma-related sanctions. This created authority to designate any foreign person operating in that sector, including persons involved in importing, exporting, selling, supplying, or transporting jet fuel in or involving Burma, without requiring proof that the fuel reached a military aircraft. Executive Order 14014 was renewed by President Trump on 3 February 2026 for a further year, and the August 2023 jet fuel sector determination remains in force. This is the most significant legal tool available to sanctions authorities for targeting the supply chain at any stage of its operation.

**Canada****'****s aviation fuel trade ban (October 2023)**

On 31 October 2023, Canada enacted Special Economic Measures (Burma) Regulations amendment SOR/2023-228, establishing a comprehensive ban on the export, sale, supply, and shipment of aviation fuel to Myanmar, and on the provision of maritime shipping insurance for vessels transporting aviation fuel to Myanmar. Canada is the first, and as of April 2026 the only, government to implement such a trade ban. The ban addresses both the commodity and the insurance dimension of the supply chain and applies to all actors regardless of individual designation status. No other government has implemented a comparable measure.

**Government designations**

Key entities designated since the coup include Asia Sun Group Company Limited (US, EU, Canada, Australia); Asia Sun Trading Company Limited (US, UK, Australia); Cargo Link Petroleum Logistics Company Limited (US, Canada, Australia); Shoon Energy Pte. Ltd. (US, UK, Canada, Australia); PEIA Pte. Ltd. and P.E.I. Energy Pte. Ltd. (US); Swan Energy Company Limited (UK, Canada); Myan Oil Company Limited (UK); Khin Phyu Win and Zaw Min Tun (US, UK, Canada); Win Kyaw Kyaw Aung (UK); REEF and NOBLE (US); Sea Route Ship Management FZE (US); Myanma Foreign Trade Bank and Myanma Investment and Commercial Bank (US, Australia, Canada); and MOGE (EU, US financial services directive). Singapore has not joined the Myanmar sanctions coalition. Vietnam has not sanctioned any entity in connection with the Myanmar aviation fuel supply chain.

**Significant unsanctioned entities**

As of April 2026, the following entities with documented roles in the supply chain have not been sanctioned in any jurisdiction: Myanma Petrochemical Enterprise (MPE); National Energy Prime Aviation Services Co. Ltd. (NEPAS); Myan Oil Terminal Company Limited; Hai Linh Co. Ltd. (Vietnam); CB Energy (Thailand); Tawan-Oil Trading (Thailand); United Amara Bank (UAB); Myanma Economic Bank (MEB). BARAAWE 1 (formerly HUITONG 78), LS MERCURY (formerly YONG SHENG 56), Abu Marine Co. Ltd., Pacific Light Operation and Ship Management, Legendsea Co. Ltd., and Allsea Management JSC have been called for sanctions by Justice for Myanmar but remain unsanctioned.

**What Advocacy Has Achieved**

The supply chain has proved resilient, but sustained investigation and advocacy have produced documented results. Several companies have withdrawn from the supply chain following engagement by Amnesty International, and government action has been taken in direct response to evidence generated by civil society investigations. The following case studies illustrate the causal relationship between advocacy and outcome.

**Thai Oil, Wilhelmsen, and Pan Ocean: direct engagement leading to withdrawal**

The most direct advocacy outcomes from the November 2022 Deadly Cargo investigation were the withdrawal of three companies from the supply chain following Amnesty International's pre-publication engagement. Thai Oil Public Company Limited suspended Myanmar aviation fuel sales on 14 October 2022, before the report's publication, after Amnesty International shared its findings. Wilhelmsen Ships Service Myanmar issued its cessation statement on 18 October 2022, four days after Thai Oil, stating it would immediately cease providing agency services for any vessel discharging Jet A-1 at Myanmar ports and that this would remain the case until further notice. Following publication, Pan Ocean Co. Ltd. committed that it would no longer allow its vessels to transport aviation fuel to Myanmar. All three companies have not been identified as parties to any documented aviation fuel shipment in the four investigations published since November 2022. The causal link between Amnesty International's direct engagement and each company's decision to exit is documented in the source record.

**Singapore: from primary supply hub to absent from the documented chain**

Singapore was the most significant load jurisdiction in Phase 1 of the supply chain. Four of the ten shipments documented in the Deadly Cargo investigation loaded at the Singapore Refining Company terminal on Jurong Island. A fifth was implicated at ExxonMobil's adjacent Jurong Island berth. A sixth departed the Vopak Singapore Banyan Terminal before reaching Vietnam in January 2024. PetroChina International Singapore Pte Ltd was identified as the seller in the first post-coup shipment in April 2021. Chevron's SRC sale (the February 2022 shipment) and SPC/PetroChina's SRC sale (the December 2021 shipment) are among the most extensively documented transactions in the supply chain record.

Following the Deadly Cargo investigation and successive sanctions rounds, Singapore has not appeared as a direct load port in any documented Myanmar aviation fuel shipment since 2024. Chevron has not been identified as a supplier in any post-2022 investigation. ExxonMobil has not been identified in any post-2022 investigation. SPC/PetroChina has not appeared. The Vopak Singapore Banyan Terminal's involvement in the January 2024 shipment was the last documented Singapore staging point. Singaporean entities continue to appear in the ownership and management structures of vessels delivering fuel in Phase 3 and in the holding companies managing the Thilawa terminal, and Singapore's government has not imposed Myanmar-related sanctions on entities registered in its jurisdiction. However, the shift of the physical supply chain away from Singapore ports represents a measurable change attributable in part to the pressure generated by successive investigations. Singapore's Foreign Minister acknowledged in February 2023 a commitment to prohibit the transfer of arms and dual-use goods to Myanmar, though comprehensive enforcement action has not followed.

**The OFAC sector determination: shifting the legal landscape**

Justice for Myanmar, Amnesty International, and civil society partners advocated consistently from November 2022 for a sanctions mechanism that would allow governments to target the supply chain without requiring proof of direct military end-use at the point of designation. This advocacy was directed explicitly at the gap created by the structure of standard sanctions regimes, which typically require evidence linking a specific entity to a specific prohibited act. The August 2023 OFAC sector determination under Executive Order 14014 addressed this gap directly, creating authority to designate any foreign person operating in Myanmar's jet fuel sector regardless of whether the fuel they supplied ultimately reached a military aircraft. The determination was followed by a pause in documented shipments from September to November 2023, suggesting it influenced supply chain behaviour. Justice for Myanmar welcomed the determination and noted it should already serve as the basis for a broader designation round.

**Canada****'****s aviation fuel ban: the most comprehensive government action**

Canada's October 2023 trade ban (SOR/2023-228) is the most comprehensive government response to the supply chain documented in reviewed sources. It prohibits not only the export of aviation fuel to Myanmar but also the provision of maritime shipping insurance for vessels transporting it, addressing a dimension of the supply chain that no other government had targeted. The ban was introduced following sustained advocacy by Canadian civil society and reflects a direct policy response to the supply chain evidence generated by successive Amnesty International investigations. Canada's designation of Asia Sun Group in December 2022 was also the first government designation of a Myanmar aviation fuel supply chain entity following the Deadly Cargo publication, preceding equivalent action by the US, UK, and EU.

**What has not changed**

Despite these outcomes, the core infrastructure of the supply chain has remained intact throughout. The Thilawa terminal continues to receive aviation fuel under the joint management of MPE and the Asia Sun/Shoon Group network. NEPAS continues to hold the import licence and purchase fuel. The financial architecture routing payments through Thai banks and private Myanmar banks has persisted, with UAB and MEB continuing to process transactions. The supply chain's adaptation from direct commercial loading to Vietnamese transit to Iranian ghost ships has not been slowed by any of the advocacy interventions documented in the source record; each intervention has produced a structural response that maintained or increased supply volumes. Documented imports reached their highest post-coup level in 2025.

**Current Status as of April 2026**

The aviation fuel supply chain to Myanmar's military is active and growing. Imports reached a post-coup high of at least 109,604 metric tonnes in 2025. 2025 was the deadliest year for aerial attacks since the coup. Airstrikes continued through the aftermath of the earthquake that struck Myanmar on 28 March 2025, with the UN reporting at least 171 airstrikes in the weeks following the disaster, including a 12 May 2025 attack in Sagaing that killed 22 students and two teachers at a school. As of late January 2026, satellite tracking data showed NOBLE near Bandar Abbas preparing for a further delivery and REEF also heading back toward Myanmar.

The supply chain has shown a consistent pattern of adaptation. When the direct commercial model was exposed in November 2022, it restructured through Vietnamese intermediaries. When that model was exposed, it shifted to Iranian-linked ghost ships using AIS manipulation. Each adaptation has maintained or increased supply volumes. The core Myanmar infrastructure comprising the Thilawa terminal, NEPAS, Cargo Link, and the Mann Refinery has remained in place throughout, under the effective control of MPE and the Asia Sun and Shoon Group network.

Justice for Myanmar, in a February 2026 statement, called on the EU, UK, Canada, US, and Australia to impose coordinated sanctions on nine named targets including MPE, Hai Linh, the four ghost fleet vessels, and their owners and managers, stating that sanctions would not only disrupt the junta's ability to continue airstrikes but would also strengthen existing measures aimed at limiting Iran's ability to export sanctioned fuel. The UN Special Rapporteur Tom Andrews, responding to Reuters' January 2026 findings, stated: 'This fuel that is being shipped in from Iran is literally fuelling mass atrocities. There has been an escalation in attacks on civilian targets. It is important to point out those that are enabling it.'